/Pg 80 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2535 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7399 0 R
/E (Contact is nodal and unilateral \(Signorini-type\): each node either stays off the obstacle with zero contact force, or touches it with a non-negative normal force.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2536 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7400 0 R
/E (Type: node against a signed-distance surface.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2537 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7401 0 R
/E (Each time step, collision detection proposes a candidate contact set C and a target point on the surface for each node in C \(the paper uses CCD\).)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7402 0 R
/E (Inside the step, the local-global loop runs with C fixed. The global step solves only for the free nodes, with the contact nodes held at their targets, which models a sticky surface and gives static friction. Sliding friction is not modelled.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7403 0 R
/E (The global matrix changes when C changes. The rows and columns of contact nodes are removed, leaving the free-node submatrix.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
2540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7404 0 R
/E (After the inner loop converges, the contact forces are recovered from the residual. Nodes whose normal force is negative are removed from C, and nodes that now penetrate the surface are added. Then a new outer iteration starts with the updated C and new targets.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
